\par
DETAILED COMMENTS (to be returned to the author(s))\par
[Reviewers are encouraged to provide substantitive comments \par
supporting their numerical scale ratings. Reviews without \par
such comments may not be considered in the overall rating of the paper.]\par

This paper provides a list of features that some CBIR systems support.
The authors argue that the existing surveys primarily focus on the general 
architecture and design aspects of the CBIR systems, and their major 
contribution is to detail which systems use what image features and 
have what functionalities.  For instance, one can read this paper and 
is able to say that systems A, B, and D support relevance feedback.

While I have to say that the authors did a good job to collect all the 
information, and especially Table 1 is very impressive, I cannot 
recommend this paper to be accepted as a research paper.  This paper 
in its current form may fit e.g., IEEE MM Magazine.  To be a competitive
research paper, I suggest the authors to do the following work:

1. Conduct extensive experiments on a popular dataset (e.g., Corel)
   and show which sets of features perform the best.

2. Or at least compare systems that employ "global" features with
   the system that employ "local" features.

3. Compare all relevance feedback methods and show which methods
   are most effective under what query scenarios.

In short, even a consumer report needs to evaluate the products
it presents to be informative and useful.  The authors should take
one step forward to conduct "product evaluation."

